where δ(xi , yi ) is the discrete metric δ(xi , yi ) =              .
                                                        0, xi = yi
Under this metric the configuration space S Z is compact, perfect and totally
disconnected, i.e. a Cantor space [11]. From now on, the configuration space
S Z endowed with this metric will be referred to as X. The ECA functions F
are continuous in X, hence (X, F ) is a (discrete) dynamical system.
    Now we introduce some key concepts of the topological dynamics treatment
of CA. A configuration x is an equicontinuity point of CA F if

       ∀ > 0, ∃δ > 0, ∀y ∈ X : d(x, y) < δ, ∀n ≥ 0 : d(F n (x), F n (y)) < .   (1)

If all configurations x ∈ X are equicontinuity points then the CA is called
equicontinuous. If there is at least one equicontinuity point, the CA is almost
equicontinuous.
    A CA is sensitive (to initial conditions) if

 ∃ > 0, ∀x ∈ X, ∀δ > 0, ∃y ∈ X : d(x, y) < δ, ∃n ≥ 0 : d(F n (x), F n (y)) ≥ . (2)

    A CA is positively expansive if

               ∃ > 0, ∀x 6= y ∈ X, ∃n ≥ 0 : d(F n (x), F n (y)) ≥ .            (3)

Positively expansive CA are sensitive [11].
    CA as dynamical systems can be classified according to a classification in-
troduced by Gilman [9] and modified by Kurka [10]. Every one-dimensional CA
falls exactly in one of the following classes [10]:
  (K1) equicontinuous CA,
  (K2) almost equicontinuous but not equicontinuous CA,
  (K3) sensitive but not positively expansive CA,
